CARDS = [
# ---------------- Technology ----------------
D(T,"ai","The test that started the conversation",
  "In 1950 Alan Turing opened a paper with a blunt question: can machines think? Rather than argue about definitions, he proposed a game. A judge exchanges typed messages with a person and a machine, without knowing which is which. If the judge cannot reliably tell them apart, Turing suggested, the machine deserves to be taken seriously. He called it the imitation game. We now call it the Turing test, and people still argue about what passing it would really mean.",
  "Wikipedia: Turing test", W+"Turing_test",
  quiz=("What did Turing originally call his test?", ["The imitation game","The thinking test","The Enigma trial"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(T,"ai","The chatbot from 1966",
  "ELIZA was a program written at MIT by Joseph Weizenbaum in the mid-1960s. Its best-known script, DOCTOR, imitated a psychotherapist by turning a person's own statements back into questions. It understood nothing; it simply matched patterns. Yet Weizenbaum was startled to find that people confided in it and grew attached to it, even when they knew how it worked. The tendency to read understanding into a machine's words is still called the ELIZA effect.",
  "Wikipedia: ELIZA", W+"ELIZA",
  quiz=("What kind of person did ELIZA's DOCTOR script imitate?", ["A psychotherapist","A librarian","A chess master"], 0)),
D(T,"ai","When a computer beat the world champion",
  "In May 1997, IBM's chess computer Deep Blue defeated the reigning world champion, Garry Kasparov, in a six-game match played under standard tournament time controls. Kasparov had beaten an earlier version of the machine the year before. The 1997 result, three and a half points to two and a half, was the first time a reigning champion lost a match to a computer under those conditions, and it became a landmark in the public story of computing.",
  "Wikipedia: Deep Blue versus Garry Kasparov", W+"Deep_Blue_versus_Garry_Kasparov",
  quiz=("In which year did Deep Blue win its match against Kasparov?", ["1987","1997","2007"], 1)),
D(T,"ai","A summer workshop named a field",
  "The phrase \"artificial intelligence\" comes from a funding proposal. In 1955 John McCarthy and a few colleagues proposed a summer research project at Dartmouth College, to be held in 1956, on the conjecture that every aspect of learning could in principle be described so precisely that a machine could simulate it. The workshop itself was loose and informal, but the name stuck, and the meeting is widely regarded as the founding event of AI as a field.",
  "Wikipedia: Dartmouth workshop", W+"Dartmouth_workshop",
  quiz=("Where was the 1956 workshop that gave AI its name?", ["Dartmouth College","Stanford University","Bell Labs"], 0)),
D(T,"ai","A program written before computers existed",
  "In 1843 Ada Lovelace published a translation of an article about Charles Babbage's proposed Analytical Engine, and added notes of her own that were longer than the article. One of them, Note G, set out step by step how the machine could calculate a sequence called the Bernoulli numbers. It is often described as the first published computer program. The engine was never completed, so the program was never run in her lifetime.",
  "Wikipedia: Ada Lovelace", W+"Ada_Lovelace",
  quiz=("Which machine was Ada Lovelace writing about?", ["The Analytical Engine","The Enigma machine","ENIAC"], 0)),
D(T,"ai","The machine that learned from mistakes",
  "In 1958 the psychologist Frank Rosenblatt demonstrated the perceptron, a simple model of a neuron that could learn to sort inputs into two groups by adjusting its own weights after each error. Newspapers of the day predicted thinking machines within years. The perceptron turned out to have real limits, and enthusiasm faded for a time. But the core idea — learning by nudging numbers after mistakes — sits underneath the neural networks used today.",
  "Wikipedia: Perceptron", W+"Perceptron",
  prompt="Think of something you learned by trial and error. How many tries did it take?"),
D(T,"products","Why the save icon is still a floppy disk",
  "Floppy disks were once how most people stored and moved their files, so early software used a picture of one to mean \"save\". The disks themselves have long since disappeared from everyday computers, yet the little square with a notch survives in toolbars. Many people who press it have never held the real thing. It's a reminder that an icon can outlive the object it was drawn from.",
  "Wikipedia: Floppy disk", W+"Floppy_disk",
  prompt="Look at your phone. Which other icons picture an object you no longer use?", evergreen=True),
D(T,"products","The mouse was made of wood",
  "The first computer mouse was built in 1964 by Bill English from a design by Douglas Engelbart at the Stanford Research Institute. It was a small wooden block with two metal wheels and a single button. Engelbart showed it to the public in December 1968, in a demonstration that also introduced on-screen windows, hypertext and video conferencing. That session is now remembered as \"the Mother of All Demos\".",
  "Wikipedia: Computer mouse", W+"Computer_mouse",
  quiz=("What was the first mouse's casing made from?", ["Wood","Aluminium","Bakelite"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(T,"products","Music you could carry",
  "Sony released the first Walkman, the TPS-L2, in Japan in July 1979. It was a cassette player that could not record and had no speaker, which some inside the company thought would doom it. Instead it sold in huge numbers and changed where music was heard: on trains, on pavements, on runs. For the first time a personal soundtrack could follow someone through a public space.",
  "Wikipedia: Walkman", W+"Walkman",
  quiz=("What did the first Walkman play?", ["Cassette tapes","Compact discs","Vinyl singles"], 0)),
D(T,"products","A king's name on your headphones",
  "Bluetooth is named after Harald Bluetooth, a tenth-century king who united parts of Denmark and Norway. An engineer suggested the name in 1997 as a temporary code name for a technology meant to unite different devices, and it was never replaced. The logo is a bind-rune that merges the king's initials: the runes Hagall and Bjarkan.",
  "Wikipedia: Bluetooth", W+"Bluetooth",
  quiz=("What does the Bluetooth logo combine?", ["Two runes","Two Greek letters","A B and a T"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(T,"products","The keyboard layout from the 1870s",
  "The QWERTY layout was developed in the early 1870s for the Sholes and Glidden typewriter, and became standard after the success of the Remington typewriters that followed. Exactly why the letters were arranged this way is still debated; one long-standing account says it helped keep the machine's metal type bars from jamming. Whatever the reason, the layout outlasted the mechanism entirely and moved onto glass screens.",
  "Wikipedia: QWERTY", W+"QWERTY",
  prompt="Type a sentence slowly and notice which hand does more of the work."),
D(T,"products","The phone announced in 2007",
  "Steve Jobs introduced the first iPhone on 9 January 2007, describing it as three products in one: a widescreen iPod with touch controls, a mobile phone, and an internet communicator. It went on sale in the United States on 29 June that year. It had no App Store at launch — that arrived in 2008 — and it could not copy and paste text until a later software update.",
  "Wikipedia: iPhone (1st generation)", W+"IPhone_(1st_generation)",
  quiz=("What was missing from the first iPhone at launch?", ["An App Store","A touchscreen","A camera"], 0)),
D(T,"internet","The first message was \"LO\"",
  "On 29 October 1969 a student programmer at UCLA, Charley Kline, tried to log in to a computer at the Stanford Research Institute over the brand-new ARPANET. He meant to type LOGIN. The system crashed after the first two letters, so the first message ever sent on the network that grew into the internet was \"LO\". The full login worked about an hour later.",
  "Wikipedia: ARPANET", W+"ARPANET",
  quiz=("What word was the sender trying to type?", ["LOGIN","LOAD","LOOK"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(T,"internet","Why email addresses have an @",
  "In 1971 the programmer Ray Tomlinson sent the first email between two different computers on the ARPANET. He needed a way to separate a person's name from the name of their machine, and chose the @ sign from the keyboard because it was unlikely to appear in anyone's name and already read as \"at\". He later said he could not remember what the first message said — probably something like a row of keys.",
  "Wikipedia: Ray Tomlinson", W+"Ray_Tomlinson",
  quiz=("Who chose the @ sign for email?", ["Ray Tomlinson","Tim Berners-Lee","Vint Cerf"], 0)),
D(T,"internet","The web was given away",
  "Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web in 1989 while working at CERN, the European physics laboratory, as a way for researchers to share linked documents. The first website went live there in 1991. In April 1993 CERN made the web's underlying software freely available to everyone, with no fees. That decision is a large part of why the web spread so quickly instead of remaining one product among many.",
  "Wikipedia: World Wide Web", W+"World_Wide_Web",
  quiz=("At which institution was the web invented?", ["CERN","MIT","NASA"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(T,"internet","The first webcam watched a coffee pot",
  "In 1991 researchers at the University of Cambridge's computer laboratory pointed a camera at the coffee pot in a room called the Trojan Room, so that people working elsewhere in the building could check whether there was any coffee before making the walk. In 1993 the image was connected to the web, where it became an early online curiosity. The camera was finally switched off in 2001.",
  "Wikipedia: Trojan Room coffee pot", W+"Trojan_Room_coffee_pot",
  quiz=("What was the first webcam pointed at?", ["A coffee pot","A fish tank","A car park"], 0)),
D(T,"internet","Emoji began as a set of 176",
  "In 1999 Shigetaka Kurita designed a set of 176 small pictures for a Japanese mobile internet service run by NTT DoCoMo. Each was drawn on a grid just twelve pixels square, and they covered weather, transport, phases of the moon and a handful of faces. The word emoji comes from the Japanese for \"picture\" and \"character\"; its resemblance to the English word emotion is a coincidence.",
  "Wikipedia: Emoji", W+"Emoji",
  quiz=("Where does the word \"emoji\" come from?", ["Japanese for picture + character","English emotion + icon","A company name"], 0)),
D(T,"general","The bug that was a moth",
  "On 9 September 1947, operators of the Harvard Mark II computer traced a fault to a moth trapped in a relay. They taped the insect into the logbook with the note \"First actual case of bug being found\". The joke only works because engineers were already calling faults \"bugs\" — Thomas Edison used the word that way in the 1870s. The logbook page, moth included, is kept by the Smithsonian.",
  "Wikipedia: Bug (engineering)", W+"Bug_(engineering)",
  quiz=("What insect was taped into the logbook?", ["A moth","A beetle","A fly"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(T,"general","A prediction that steered an industry",
  "In 1965 Gordon Moore, later a co-founder of Intel, observed that the number of components on an integrated circuit had been doubling every year, and predicted that this would continue for a decade. In 1975 he revised the pace to a doubling about every two years. Moore's law was never a law of nature. It was an observation that became a target, and chip makers planned around it for decades.",
  "Wikipedia: Moore's law", W+"Moore%27s_law",
  quiz=("What does Moore's law describe doubling?", ["Components on a chip","Internet speed","Battery life"], 0)),
D(T,"general","Your map needs Einstein",
  "GPS receivers work out where they are by comparing time signals from satellites. The clocks on those satellites do not tick at quite the same rate as clocks on the ground: moving fast slows them slightly, while weaker gravity at altitude speeds them up by more. The net effect is that they run ahead by about 38 microseconds a day. Without correcting for relativity, positions would drift by kilometres within a day.",
  "Wikipedia: Error analysis for the Global Positioning System", W+"Error_analysis_for_the_Global_Positioning_System",
  quiz=("Left uncorrected, satellite clocks would run…", ["Ahead of ground clocks","Behind ground clocks","Exactly in step"], 0)),

# ---------------- Design ----------------
D(DS,None,"The typeface that tried to say nothing",
  "Helvetica was created in 1957 by Max Miedinger with Eduard Hoffmann at the Haas type foundry in Switzerland. It was first sold as Neue Haas Grotesk and renamed a few years later, after the Latin name for Switzerland, to help it sell abroad. Its designers wanted a clear, neutral typeface with no particular character of its own. That neutrality made it one of the most widely used typefaces of the twentieth century.",
  "Wikipedia: Helvetica", W+"Helvetica",
  quiz=("What was Helvetica first called?", ["Neue Haas Grotesk","Swiss Modern","Akzidenz"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(DS,None,"The map that ignored geography",
  "Harry Beck was a draughtsman who, in 1931, redrew the London Underground map as a diagram. He straightened the lines, allowed only horizontal, vertical and 45-degree angles, and spaced the stations evenly regardless of the real distances between them. His reasoning was that passengers underground only need to know the order of stations and where to change. First printed for the public in 1933, it became the model for transit maps around the world.",
  "Wikipedia: Tube map", W+"Tube_map",
  prompt="Next time you read a transit map, notice what it leaves out.", evergreen=True),
D(DS,None,"As little design as possible",
  "Dieter Rams led design at the German company Braun for decades and summed up his approach in ten principles of good design. Good design, he wrote, is innovative, useful, aesthetic, understandable, unobtrusive, honest, long-lasting, thorough down to the last detail, environmentally friendly — and, finally, as little design as possible. His calculators, radios and shelving have been cited as an influence by many later product designers.",
  "Wikipedia: Dieter Rams", W+"Dieter_Rams",
  prompt="Pick up an object near you. Which of its details could be removed without loss?", evergreen=True),
D(DS,None,"A school that lasted fourteen years",
  "The Bauhaus was a German school of art and design founded by the architect Walter Gropius in Weimar in 1919. It set out to bring fine art, craft and industry together, and its students worked in workshops as well as studios. The school moved to Dessau and then Berlin before closing under pressure from the Nazi regime in 1933. Its teachers scattered across the world, and carried its ideas with them.",
  "Wikipedia: Bauhaus", W+"Bauhaus",
  quiz=("In which city was the Bauhaus founded?", ["Weimar","Vienna","Zurich"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"Why screen corners are easy to hit",
  "In 1954 the psychologist Paul Fitts described a relationship now known as Fitts's law: the time it takes to move to a target depends on how far away it is and how big it is. Large, close targets are fastest. Interface designers lean on this constantly. On a computer, the edges and corners of the screen stop the pointer, which in effect makes targets placed there very large — and very quick to reach.",
  "Wikipedia: Fitts's law", W+"Fitts%27s_law",
  quiz=("According to Fitts's law, which target is quickest to reach?", ["A large, nearby one","A small, nearby one","A large, distant one"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"The paths people actually take",
  "A desire path is the worn track that appears where people repeatedly walk across grass rather than follow the paved route. It usually marks the shortest or easiest way between two points. Some planners treat these tracks as a nuisance; others treat them as free research, waiting to see where paths appear before deciding where to lay paving. Either way, they are a record of what people actually do.",
  "Wikipedia: Desire path", W+"Desire_path",
  prompt="Look for a desire path on your next walk. Where does it lead?", evergreen=True),
D(DS,None,"A sign you can read from behind",
  "The stop sign's eight-sided shape was chosen in the United States in the early 1920s. An unusual outline has practical advantages: the sign can be recognised at night or when its face is obscured, and drivers approaching from the opposite direction can identify it from the back and know that others have to stop. The signs were not always red; yellow was common until a 1954 standard changed the colour.",
  "Wikipedia: Stop sign", W+"Stop_sign",
  quiz=("What colour were many early stop signs?", ["Yellow","Blue","Green"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"Designs that remember their past",
  "A skeuomorph is a feature carried over from an older version of an object, where it was necessary, into a newer one, where it is only decoration. Rivets printed on jeans pockets, a camera shutter sound on a phone, and a notes app styled like yellow lined paper are all examples. Such cues can make something new feel familiar, which is why early touchscreen software used so many of them.",
  "Wikipedia: Skeuomorph", W+"Skeuomorph",
  prompt="Find one skeuomorph within arm's reach."),
D(DS,None,"A common language for colour",
  "In 1963 Lawrence Herbert introduced the Pantone Matching System, a book of standardised, numbered colours. Before it, a designer and a printer could easily disagree about what \"the same red\" meant. With a shared reference, a colour could be specified by number and reproduced by different printers in different places. The system is still used to keep brand colours consistent across packaging, print and products.",
  "Wikipedia: Pantone", W+"Pantone",
  quiz=("What problem did Pantone's system solve?", ["Matching colours between printers","Mixing paint cheaply","Naming new colours"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"Pictures instead of words",
  "In 1920s Vienna the social scientist Otto Neurath, working with Marie Reidemeister and the artist Gerd Arntz, developed Isotype, a method of showing statistics with rows of simple repeated pictograms. A larger quantity was shown by more symbols rather than by a bigger symbol, so that amounts could be compared at a glance. Its clean figures are an ancestor of the pictograms used on signs in airports and stations today.",
  "Wikipedia: Isotype (picture language)", W+"Isotype_(picture_language)",
  quiz=("How did Isotype show a larger quantity?", ["More symbols","A bigger symbol","A darker symbol"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"The Olympic figures on a grid",
  "For the 1972 Olympic Games in Munich, the German designer Otl Aicher led a team that created a complete visual identity, including a set of pictograms for each sport. The figures were built on a strict grid of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines, which made them consistent and easy to read without any language. They became one of the most influential pictogram systems ever made.",
  "Wikipedia: Otl Aicher", W+"Otl_Aicher",
  prompt="Spot a pictogram today. Could you understand it with no words at all?"),
D(DS,None,"A logo sketched in a taxi",
  "The I ♥ NY logo was created by the graphic designer Milton Glaser in the mid-1970s for a campaign to promote tourism in New York State. He is said to have sketched the idea in the back of a taxi, and he did the work without a fee, expecting the campaign to run for only a few months. It became one of the most imitated designs in the world.",
  "Wikipedia: I Love New York", W+"I_Love_New_York",
  quiz=("Who designed the I ♥ NY logo?", ["Milton Glaser","Paul Rand","Saul Bass"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"Doors that need instructions",
  "In The Design of Everyday Things, first published in 1988, Don Norman argued that when people struggle to use an object, the fault usually lies with the design and not with them. A door that needs a \"push\" sign has failed to show how it works. Such doors are now nicknamed Norman doors. A flat plate suggests pushing; a handle suggests pulling. Good design makes the right action the obvious one.",
  "Wikipedia: The Design of Everyday Things", W+"The_Design_of_Everyday_Things",
  prompt="Notice the next door that confuses you. What would fix it?", evergreen=True),
D(DS,None,"Why A4 folds so neatly",
  "The A series of paper sizes is built on one ratio: the long side is the short side multiplied by the square root of two. That is the only proportion where cutting a sheet in half across its length gives two sheets with the same shape as the original. The largest size, A0, has an area of one square metre. Halve it four times and you arrive at A4.",
  "Wikipedia: ISO 216", W+"ISO_216",
  quiz=("What is the area of a sheet of A0?", ["One square metre","Half a square metre","Two square metres"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(DS,None,"The typeface made for a cartoon dog",
  "Comic Sans was designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994. He had seen a test version of a friendly software guide in which a cartoon dog spoke in speech bubbles set in Times New Roman, and thought the formal typeface looked wrong. He drew a casual alternative based on the lettering in comic books. It was finished too late for that program but shipped with Windows soon afterwards.",
  "Wikipedia: Comic Sans", W+"Comic_Sans",
  quiz=("What inspired the letterforms of Comic Sans?", ["Comic book lettering","Children's handwriting","Chalkboard menus"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"Three lines from 1981",
  "The three stacked lines known as the hamburger icon were drawn by the designer Norm Cox for the Xerox Star, a pioneering office computer released in 1981. It marked a menu and was meant to resemble a small list. The symbol then largely vanished for decades before returning on smartphone screens, where space is tight and a tiny, recognisable menu button became valuable again.",
  "Wikipedia: Hamburger button", W+"Hamburger_button",
  quiz=("Which computer first used the hamburger icon?", ["Xerox Star","Apple Lisa","Commodore 64"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"The space between two letters",
  "Kerning is the adjustment of space between particular pairs of letters so that text looks evenly spaced. Some pairs, such as A and V, leave an awkward gap if each letter simply sits in its own box, so type designers build in corrections for them. Good kerning is invisible. Bad kerning can make one word read as two, which is why designers adjust large headlines by eye.",
  "Wikipedia: Kerning", W+"Kerning",
  prompt="Look at a shop sign or headline. Are any two letters too far apart?"),
D(DS,None,"A lamp balanced on springs",
  "The Anglepoise lamp was designed in 1932 by George Carwardine, an English engineer who specialised in vehicle suspension. He had developed a spring mechanism that could hold a jointed arm in any position without clamps, working rather like the tension of muscles in a human arm, and went looking for a use for it. A desk lamp that stays exactly where it is put turned out to be the answer.",
  "Wikipedia: Anglepoise lamp", W+"Anglepoise_lamp",
  quiz=("What was the designer's specialism?", ["Vehicle suspension","Watchmaking","Aircraft wings"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"A student drew the recycling symbol",
  "The three chasing arrows of the recycling symbol were designed in 1970 by Gary Anderson, then a 23-year-old student, for a competition run by a packaging company around the time of the first Earth Day. His design is based on the Möbius strip, a loop with a single continuous side. The symbol was placed in the public domain, which is why it appears freely on products everywhere.",
  "Wikipedia: Recycling symbol", W+"Recycling_symbol",
  quiz=("Which shape is the recycling symbol based on?", ["A Möbius strip","A triangle knot","A spiral"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"A chair meant to feel like a baseball glove",
  "Charles and Ray Eames released their lounge chair and ottoman in 1956. Made of moulded plywood shells and leather cushions, it was a departure for designers known for affordable, mass-produced furniture. Charles Eames said he wanted it to have the warm, receptive look of a well-used first baseman's mitt. The chair has been in continuous production ever since and is held in the collection of the Museum of Modern Art.",
  "Wikipedia: Eames Lounge Chair", W+"Eames_Lounge_Chair",
  quiz=("What did Charles Eames compare the chair's look to?", ["A baseball mitt","A sailing boat","An armchair in a club"], 0)),
D(DS,None,"Grouping without boxes",
  "In the early twentieth century a group of German psychologists described how people naturally organise what they see. Their Gestalt principles are still taught to designers. Things placed close together are seen as a group, which is the principle of proximity. Things that look alike are seen as related, which is similarity. A designer can therefore show that items belong together using only space and likeness, without drawing a single border.",
  "Wikipedia: Principles of grouping", W+"Principles_of_grouping",
  prompt="Open any app. What tells you which items belong together — lines, or just space?"),

# ---------------- Books ----------------
D(B,"fiction","A monster born from a rainy summer",
  "In the cold, wet summer of 1816, a group of writers staying near Lake Geneva passed the time by challenging each other to write a ghost story. One of them was Mary Godwin, soon to be Mary Shelley, who was eighteen. Her idea grew into Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus, published anonymously in 1818. It is often described as one of the first works of science fiction.",
  "Wikipedia: Frankenstein", W+"Frankenstein",
  quiz=("How did Frankenstein begin?", ["A ghost-story challenge","A newspaper serial","A stage play"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(B,"fiction","The knight who read too much",
  "Don Quixote, by Miguel de Cervantes, was published in two parts, in 1605 and 1615. Its hero is a gentleman who reads so many tales of chivalry that he decides to become a knight himself, riding out with a farmer, Sancho Panza, as his squire. It is frequently called the first modern novel, and it gave English the word quixotic for ideals that are noble and impractical.",
  "Wikipedia: Don Quixote", W+"Don_Quixote",
  quiz=("Who is Don Quixote's squire?", ["Sancho Panza","Rocinante","Dulcinea"], 0)),
D(B,"fiction","A novel a thousand years old",
  "The Tale of Genji was written in the early eleventh century by Murasaki Shikibu, a lady-in-waiting at the Japanese imperial court. Across fifty-four chapters it follows the life and loves of a prince and the generation after him, with close attention to its characters' inner feelings. It is often described as the world's first novel, and it is still read, studied and adapted in Japan today.",
  "Wikipedia: The Tale of Genji", W+"The_Tale_of_Genji",
  quiz=("Who wrote The Tale of Genji?", ["Murasaki Shikibu","Sei Shōnagon","Matsuo Bashō"], 0)),
D(B,"fiction","A first line written on an exam paper",
  "J. R. R. Tolkien was a professor at Oxford, marking examination papers one summer, when he came across a page a student had left blank. On it he wrote: \"In a hole in the ground there lived a hobbit.\" He did not yet know what a hobbit was. The story that grew from that sentence was published as The Hobbit in 1937.",
  "Wikipedia: The Hobbit", W+"The_Hobbit",
  prompt="Write one opening sentence today, with no idea what comes next.", evergreen=True),
D(B,"fiction","A classic written with fifty words",
  "Green Eggs and Ham, published in 1960, uses just fifty different words. Its author, Theodor Geisel — Dr. Seuss — wrote it after his publisher, Bennett Cerf, bet him that he could not write a book with so small a vocabulary. His earlier book The Cat in the Hat had used 236. The restriction did the story no harm: it became one of the best-selling children's books in English.",
  "Wikipedia: Green Eggs and Ham", W+"Green_Eggs_and_Ham",
  quiz=("How many different words are in Green Eggs and Ham?", ["50","150","236"], 0)),
D(B,"fiction","A book to try: The Remains of the Day",
  "Kazuo Ishiguro's 1989 novel is narrated by Stevens, an English butler taking a rare motoring holiday in the 1950s. As he drives, he looks back over decades of loyal service and what it may have cost him. Very little is said outright; the story lives in what Stevens cannot quite admit. The novel won the Booker Prize, and Ishiguro was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2017.",
  "Wikipedia: The Remains of the Day", W+"The_Remains_of_the_Day",
  prompt="If it sounds like your kind of book, add it to your list."),
D(B,"fiction","The line Holmes never said",
  "\"Elementary, my dear Watson\" is perhaps the most famous thing Sherlock Holmes says. Yet the phrase does not appear in any of the sixty Holmes stories written by Arthur Conan Doyle. Holmes does say \"Elementary\" and he does say \"my dear Watson\", but never together. The combined line became attached to the character later, through stage and screen versions.",
  "Wikipedia: Sherlock Holmes", W+"Sherlock_Holmes",
  quiz=("How many Holmes stories did Conan Doyle write?", ["60","24","112"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(B,"fiction","By the author of Sense and Sensibility",
  "Pride and Prejudice appeared in 1813 without Jane Austen's name on it. The title page said only that it was by the author of Sense and Sensibility — a book that had itself been credited simply to \"A Lady\". Austen had drafted an early version in the 1790s under the title First Impressions. None of her novels carried her name during her lifetime.",
  "Wikipedia: Pride and Prejudice", W+"Pride_and_Prejudice",
  quiz=("What was the novel's early working title?", ["First Impressions","Elizabeth","Pemberley"], 0)),
D(B,"fiction","A village where it rains for years",
  "One Hundred Years of Solitude, by the Colombian writer Gabriel García Márquez, was published in 1967. It follows seven generations of the Buendía family in the invented town of Macondo, where extraordinary events are told in the same calm voice as ordinary ones. The novel is a landmark of the style known as magic realism. García Márquez received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1982.",
  "Wikipedia: One Hundred Years of Solitude", W+"One_Hundred_Years_of_Solitude",
  quiz=("What is the name of the novel's town?", ["Macondo","Comala","Santa Teresa"], 0)),
D(B,"fiction","A library containing every book",
  "In 1941 the Argentine writer Jorge Luis Borges published a short story called The Library of Babel. It imagines a universe made of hexagonal rooms lined with books that contain every possible combination of letters. Somewhere on its shelves is every book that could ever be written — and an overwhelming amount of nonsense. Its librarians spend their lives searching. The story runs to only a few pages.",
  "Wikipedia: The Library of Babel", W+"The_Library_of_Babel",
  prompt="Short stories fit into small gaps. Could you read one this week?"),
D(B,"nonfiction","A book to try: The Making of Prince of Persia",
  "In the 1980s Jordan Mechner, just out of university, kept a journal while he designed and programmed the game Prince of Persia. He later published those journals, covering 1985 to 1993. They record the doubts, false starts and small breakthroughs of making something new, including filming his younger brother running and jumping so that he could trace the footage into the game's lifelike animation.",
  "Wikipedia: Prince of Persia (1989 video game)", W+"Prince_of_Persia_(1989_video_game)",
  prompt="Would a creator's diary interest you? Add it to your list.", evergreen=True),
D(B,"nonfiction","About 180 copies changed everything",
  "In the 1450s, in the German city of Mainz, Johannes Gutenberg completed a Bible printed with movable metal type. Around 180 copies are thought to have been made, some on paper and some on vellum. It was the first major book produced in Europe by this method, and it showed that books could be made in quantity. Forty-nine copies, complete or partial, are known to survive.",
  "Wikipedia: Gutenberg Bible", W+"Gutenberg_Bible",
  quiz=("In which city was the Gutenberg Bible printed?", ["Mainz","Venice","Paris"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(B,"nonfiction","The dictionary that took generations",
  "Work on what became the Oxford English Dictionary began in the 1850s, and its first edition was not completed until 1928. It was built from millions of quotation slips posted in by volunteer readers. One of the most prolific contributors, William Chester Minor, was a retired American army surgeon who sent his slips from Broadmoor, a secure psychiatric hospital where he was a patient.",
  "Wikipedia: Oxford English Dictionary", W+"Oxford_English_Dictionary",
  quiz=("How was the dictionary's evidence gathered?", ["Slips from volunteer readers","Interviews","Royal archives"], 0)),
D(B,"nonfiction","Good books for the price of cigarettes",
  "In 1935 the publisher Allen Lane launched Penguin Books with ten paperback reprints priced at sixpence each. The story goes that he had found nothing worth reading at a railway station bookstall. Paperbacks existed already, but they were mostly of low quality. Penguin's idea was that good writing, cleanly designed, could be sold cheaply and in places other than bookshops. It sold millions within a year.",
  "Wikipedia: Penguin Books", W+"Penguin_Books",
  quiz=("How much did the first Penguins cost?", ["Sixpence","One shilling","Half a crown"], 0)),
D(B,"nonfiction","A book to try: Silent Spring",
  "Rachel Carson was a marine biologist and a gifted writer. Her 1962 book Silent Spring described how synthetic pesticides, used widely and with little caution, were moving through the natural world and harming birds, fish and other wildlife. It was attacked by chemical companies and read by millions. The book is widely credited with helping to start the modern environmental movement.",
  "Wikipedia: Silent Spring", W+"Silent_Spring",
  quiz=("What was Rachel Carson's profession?", ["Marine biologist","Journalist","Chemist"], 0)),
D(B,"nonfiction","A diary in shorthand",
  "Samuel Pepys, a naval administrator in London, kept a private diary from 1660 to 1669. He wrote it in shorthand, and it was not deciphered and published until the nineteenth century. It is one of the best eyewitness accounts of its age, covering the plague of 1665 and the Great Fire of 1666 — during which Pepys buried his wine and a Parmesan cheese in the garden to keep them safe.",
  "Wikipedia: Samuel Pepys", W+"Samuel_Pepys",
  quiz=("What did Pepys bury during the Great Fire?", ["Wine and cheese","His diary","Gold coins"], 0), evergreen=True),
D(B,"nonfiction","The numbers on the spine",
  "In 1876 the American librarian Melvil Dewey published a system that divides knowledge into ten main classes, each given a range of numbers, with decimals for ever finer subjects. Because a book's number describes its subject and not its position on a particular shelf, a library can grow without renumbering anything. The Dewey Decimal Classification is still used by libraries in many countries.",
  "Wikipedia: Dewey Decimal Classification", W+"Dewey_Decimal_Classification",
  prompt="Next time you're in a library, find where your favourite subject lives."),
D(B,"nonfiction","A birthday present became a record",
  "Anne Frank was given a red-and-white checked autograph book for her thirteenth birthday in June 1942, and began using it as a diary. Weeks later her family went into hiding in Amsterdam, and she kept writing for just over two years. Her father, Otto, the only member of the family to survive, arranged for the diary to be published in 1947. It has since been translated into dozens of languages.",
  "Wikipedia: The Diary of a Young Girl", W+"The_Diary_of_a_Young_Girl",
  quiz=("When was the diary first published?", ["1947","1942","1960"], 0)),
D(B,"nonfiction","The man who invented the essay",
  "In 1580 the French writer Michel de Montaigne published a collection of short prose pieces on subjects from friendship to thumbs. He called them Essais — attempts, or trials — because each was a try at thinking something through, with himself as the test case. The form and its name both stuck. Whenever someone writes a personal, exploratory piece of prose, they are following Montaigne.",
  "Wikipedia: Essays (Montaigne)", W+"Essays_(Montaigne)",
  quiz=("What does the French word \"essai\" mean?", ["An attempt","A letter","A lesson"], 0)),
D(B,"nonfiction","A book to try: A Short History of Nearly Everything",
  "Bill Bryson is best known as a travel writer. In 2003 he published a book born of his own curiosity about how scientists know what they know: the age of the universe, the weight of the Earth, what is inside an atom. He tells the story through the often eccentric people who worked these things out. It won the Aventis Prize for general science books in 2004.",
  "Wikipedia: A Short History of Nearly Everything", W+"A_Short_History_of_Nearly_Everything",
  prompt="Curious about how we know what we know? Add it to your list."),
]
